// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/syscall/table.vuma
// VWK Syscall Table — 512-entry handler registry (PMT-pure).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the x86_64 syscall handler table. It maintains a 512-entry
// handler table (one slot per asm-generic syscall number 0..511, each slot
// holding a u64 handler-function address or 0 if unregistered) and
// provides the register / lookup API the dispatch layer (sibling
// dispatch.vuma) consumes.
//
// ── Why 512 entries? ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Linux asm-generic/unistd.h (the canonical modern Linux syscall ABI, used
// by VUMA — see womb/syscalls.vuma) currently numbers syscalls 0..449+
// (the highest number as of kernel 6.x is around 449 = clone3). The table
// is sized to 512 to give comfortable headroom for future syscalls without
// a resize — and 512 happens to be a power of 2 (cheap modulo for
// bounds-checking if needed). K11 may shrink this to 256 if memory is
// tight; the API is size-agnostic (every helper bounds-checks nr against
// the caller's expectation, not against an internal constant — see
// "Why does this file not bounds-check nr?" below).
//
// ── Why a flat [u8; 4096] array? ────────────────────────────────────────────
// The handler table is stored as a flat [u8; 4096] array (512 × 8 bytes,
// each u64 handler address packed little-endian) rather than [u64; 512].
// This is the SAME codegen limitation that forces:
//   - womb/kernel/mm/pmm.vuma (K2a) to store its FreeNode pool as three
//     parallel [u8; N] arrays (bases, orders, nexts)
//   - womb/kernel/mm/kmalloc.vuma (K2f) to store its slab freelist as a
//     flat [u8; N] array
//   - womb/kernel/trap/irq.vuma (K3d) to store its IrqTable as [u8; 2048]
//     (256 × 8 bytes — same structure, just half the entries)
// The codegen only lowers `state.array[idx]` correctly for [u8; N]
// arrays (src/pipeline.rs::flatten_expr, Expr::Index arm — non-u8 element
// types don't get the index-scaled-by-element-size treatment). See
// pmm.vuma's "Why a flat byte-array pool?" header for the full rationale.
//
// The pack/unpack helpers (syscall_table_get / syscall_table_set) are
// byte-identical in structure to pmm.vuma's pool_get_base / pool_set_base
// (K2a), kmalloc.vuma's slab helpers (K2f), and irq.vuma's
// irq_get_handler / irq_set_handler (K3d): 8-iteration while loop, shift
// by i*8, mask 255 on writes, sum-of-shifted-bytes on reads.
//
// ── Syscall handler lifecycle ───────────────────────────────────────────────
//   1. kmain allocates a SyscallTable via state_new(SyscallTable), zero-
//      inits it (count = 0, all handler slots = 0). [K11 — early boot]
//   2. Subsystems call syscall_register(tbl, nr, handler) to install a
//      handler for their syscall(s). syscall_register:
//        a. Packs the handler address into tbl.handlers[nr*8..nr*8+8]
//           via syscall_table_set.
//        b. Increments tbl.count.
//      (No "unregister" in this wave; K11 may add one if needed for
//      runtime syscall-table patching / seccomp-style filtering.)
//   3. On a syscall (int 0x80 on x86_64), trap_entry saves the TrapFrame
//      and calls trap_handler (sibling trap.vuma). The dispatcher routes
//      vector 128 to trap_syscall, which (in K11) calls:
//        a. syscall_args_from_frame(tf, args) — sibling abi.vuma
//        b. syscall_dispatch(tbl, args)       — sibling dispatch.vuma
//        c. syscall_write_ret(tf, ret)        — sibling abi.vuma
//      syscall_dispatch looks up the handler via syscall_table_get,
//      calls it (K11 — VUMA has no fn-pointer call today, so the
//      dispatch's handler-call is a stub; K11 adds real dispatch), and
//      returns the handler's return value.
//
// ── Why is syscall_register's count increment one-shot (no decrement)? ─────
// The K3e contract specifies `syscall_register(tbl, nr, handler)` only —
// no `syscall_unregister`. `tbl.count` is a "how many syscalls are
// registered" counter for diagnostics (kmain can print it during boot
// to confirm the syscall table was populated correctly); it's incremented
// by syscall_register and never decremented. Same pattern as K3d's
// irq.vuma register_irq (no unregister_irq). K11 may add unregister
// helpers if runtime syscall-table patching becomes necessary.
//
// ── Why does this file not bounds-check nr? ────────────────────────────────
// syscall_table_get / syscall_table_set do NOT check `nr < 512`. The
// dispatch layer (sibling dispatch.vuma) is responsible for bounds-
// checking nr (it returns -ENOSYS = 38 for nr >= 512 BEFORE calling
// syscall_table_get). This avoids a redundant bounds check on every
// lookup (the dispatch layer already did one) and keeps the table
// helpers simple (just pack/unpack, no policy). K3d's irq.vuma follows
// the same pattern (irq_get_handler / irq_set_handler don't bounds-check
// `irq` either — the caller is responsible).
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. The
// SyscallTable is a 4100-byte struct (4096-byte handlers array + 4-byte
// count) held in a `___pmt_buffer`-backed State<SyscallTable>; the
// CALLER allocates it via `state_new(SyscallTable)` and passes it by
// reference to syscall_register / syscall_table_get / syscall_table_set
// (init-style API — see "Why init-style?" in womb/kernel/mm/pmm.vuma for
// the codegen rationale). The State<SyscallTable> params are regular
// VUMA-fn borrows (NOT extern #[borrow] — VUMA fn calls don't need the
// attribute, only extern "C" calls do). None of this file's functions
// call any extern, so the codegen's conservative State-invalidation-on-
// extern-call doesn't fire.
//
// ── Decimal-constants discipline ────────────────────────────────────────────
// All non-zero constants in this file use DECIMAL per the K3e contract's
// "IMPORTANT" rule (8192 not 0x2000, 4096 not 0x1000):
//     4096 = 0x1000 = 512 entries × 8 bytes (handler-table array size)
//     8192 = 0x2000 = the dummy handler address used in the self-test
//                     (also confirms u64 round-trip through pack/unpack
//                      for a value larger than 255, catching any
//                      accidental u8 truncation in the codegen's [u8; N]
//                      access lowering — same rationale as K3a's
//                      rip=4096 check and K3d's irq handler=4096 check)
//     8    = bytes per u64 handler entry (loop bound for pack/unpack)
//     255  = 0xFF (byte mask for the unpack-shift-and-truncate path)
//     64   = asm-generic __NR_write (the syscall number used in the
//            self-test — write is the canonical "hello world" syscall)
// Decimal avoids the parser's hex-literal path (parse_int_radix width-
// extension subtleties — see womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for
// the same rationale, and K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma for the same rule).
//
// ── VUMA has no `import` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This file is self-contained. It declares the SyscallTable layout for
// the FIRST time (no prior VUMA file has it) and provides the table
// helpers. Future files that want to call syscall_register / table_get /
// table_set will re-declare the SyscallTable layout byte-identically to
// this file (same pattern as K3d's irq.vuma declaring IrqTable and
// future files re-declaring it). The sibling dispatch.vuma re-declares
// SyscallTable byte-identically to this file.
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/syscall/table.vuma \
//         /tmp/table.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/table.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0 (3 self-test checks all pass — handler
// round-trip for write at nr=64, count increment to 1, unregistered
// nr=0 reads back as 0).

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layouts.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Syscall table: 512 entries (asm-generic syscall numbers 0..511).
// Each entry is a handler function address (u64) or 0 if unregistered.
// Uses flat [u8; 4096] (512 * 8 bytes) with pack/unpack helpers — see
// file header "Why a flat [u8; 4096] array?" for the codegen rationale.
// `count` tracks how many handlers are registered (incremented by
// syscall_register, never decremented — see "Why is syscall_register's
// count increment one-shot?" in the file header).
layout SyscallTable = {
    handlers: [u8; 4096],  // 512 * 8 bytes — u64 handler addr per syscall
    count: u32,
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Byte-array pack/unpack helpers for SyscallTable.handlers.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Byte-identical in structure to pmm.vuma's pool_get_base / pool_set_base
// (K2a), kmalloc.vuma's slab helpers (K2f), and irq.vuma's
// irq_get_handler / irq_set_handler (K3d): 8-iteration while loop,
// shift by i*8, mask 255 on writes, sum-of-shifted-bytes on reads. The
// only difference is the index type: this file takes u32 (syscall
// numbers are u32 in syscall_register; the dispatch layer casts the u64
// `args.nr` down to u32 after the nr >= 512 bounds check).

// Read the u64 handler address for syscall `nr` from SyscallTable.handlers.
// Returns 0 if no handler is registered for that syscall (the slot is all
// zero bytes — syscall_register hasn't been called for that syscall yet,
// or the syscall was never registered in the first place). The caller
// (dispatch layer) is responsible for bounds-checking nr BEFORE calling
// this — see "Why does this file not bounds-check nr?" in the file header.
fn syscall_table_get(tbl: State<SyscallTable>, nr: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = nr * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.handlers[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 handler address for syscall `nr` into SyscallTable.handlers.
// Packs the 8 bytes little-endian (byte 0 = bits 7..0, byte 7 = bits
// 63..56). The mask-255 truncates each shifted byte to its low 8 bits
// (the (handler >> sh) >> 8 path would lose the high bits — same
// pattern as pmm.vuma's pool_set_base).
fn syscall_table_set(tbl: State<SyscallTable>, nr: u32, handler: u64) {
    let off = nr * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (handler >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.handlers[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Syscall lifecycle: register.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Register a syscall handler.
//   1. Pack the handler address into tbl.handlers[nr*8..nr*8+8] via
//      syscall_table_set.
//   2. Increment tbl.count.
// Unlike K3d's register_irq, this function does NOT call any extern
// (no PIC to unmask — syscalls are software traps, not hardware IRQs).
// K11 may add a "syscall table commit" extern here if the bare-metal
// kernel uses a copy-on-write syscall table for hot-patching.
fn syscall_register(tbl: State<SyscallTable>, nr: u32, handler: u64) {
    syscall_table_set(tbl, nr, handler);
    tbl.count = tbl.count + 1;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Allocate a SyscallTable via state_new, zero-init count, then:
//   (1) Register a handler for syscall 64 (__NR_write — the canonical
//       "hello world" syscall) at address 8192. syscall_register should
//       pack the address into the table and increment count.
//   (2) Verify the handler round-trips: syscall_table_get(tbl, 64) returns
//       8192. Catches any pack/unpack byte-order bug AND any u8-vs-u64
//       truncation bug (8192 > 255, so a u8-truncating codegen path
//       would return 0).
//   (3) Verify count was incremented to 1.
//   (4) Verify an unregistered syscall (nr=0, which is __NR_io_setup on
//       asm-generic — typically not registered in early boot) reads back
//       as 0.
// Returns 0 on success, 1 on handler round-trip failure, 2 on count
// failure, 3 on unregistered-slot failure (so a future CI failure
// pinpoints the broken check).
//
// Per the K3e contract's "IMPORTANT" rule: all non-zero constants in the
// self-test use DECIMAL (64, 8192) — decimal avoids the parser's hex-
// literal path (parse_int_radix width-extension subtleties — see
// womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same rationale, and
// K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma for the same rule applied to vector=14
// and rip=4096).

fn main() -> i32 {
    let tbl = state_new(SyscallTable);
    tbl.count = 0;

    // Register a handler for syscall 64 (__NR_write).
    syscall_register(tbl, 64, 8192);

    // Check 1: handler round-trips (8192 > 255 — catches u8 truncation).
    if syscall_table_get(tbl, 64) != 8192 { return 1; }

    // Check 2: count was incremented.
    if tbl.count != 1 { return 2; }

    // Check 3: unregistered syscall → 0.
    if syscall_table_get(tbl, 0) != 0 { return 3; }

    return 0;
}
